igns with standard local development practices: it installs an existing version of PostgreSQL (`postgresql@17`), generates a standard self-signed certificate and key directly in the data directory, verifies the connection with `psql` using `sslmode=require`, and configures Django properly. 

In contrast, Reply 1 suggests and repeatedly hardcodes an unreleased version (`postgresql@18`), which will cause installation commands to fail for a beginner. It also unnecessarily overcomplicates the SSL process by building a full local Certificate Authority infrastructure (CA, CSR, SAN extensions, and signing steps) when a self-signed certificate is standard for local development.

